meta {
  name: Log Activity (Call)
  type: http
  seq: 11
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/activities
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

body:json {
  {
    "leadId": "{{hotLeadId}}",
    "type": "call"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("returns a timeline-ready row", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.activity.type).to.equal("call");
    expect(body.activity).to.have.property("occurredAt");
    expect(body.activity).to.have.property("userName");
  });

  test("defaults the summary — a call stands on its own", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().activity.summary).to.equal("Call logged");
  });

  test("echoes the lead's bumped timestamp", function () {
    expect(res.getBody()).to.have.property("leadLastActivityAt");
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /activities — log a touch on a lead (PAC-16)

  One endpoint behind two surfaces: the Producer Dashboard's Call / Text / Email
  quick actions, and the Lead Detail note composer. They differ only in `type`.

  - **Module:** `leads` — logging a touch on a lead is a lead write.
  - **Permission:** `leads:write`
  - **Data scope:** the lead must be reachable by the caller. Another producer's
    lead is a **404**, not a 403 — whether it exists is not the caller's business.

  > **This records a touch; it does not perform one.** The client's
  > `tel:` / `sms:` / `mailto:` link places the call — this call says the
  > producer made it. Nothing here integrates with a phone system.

  ## Body
  | field | type | rules |
  |-------|------|-------|
  | leadId | string | required |
  | type | enum | required — `call` \| `text` \| `email` \| `note` |
  | summary | string | 1–500 chars. **Required for `note`**; defaulted otherwise |
  | occurredAt | date | optional, for backdating a touch made away from the app |

  ### The write vocabulary is narrower than the read vocabulary
  `ACTIVITY_TYPES` also contains `lead_created`, `quoted`, `sold` and
  `audit_resolved`. Those are **system-generated provenance**, each written by
  the pipeline that owns the event, and a client cannot post them — see
  `Log Activity (Invalid Type)`. This is a security boundary: a forged `sold`
  row would invent a sale on the Sold scorecard and the Leaderboard.

  ## Side effects
  - The lead's `lastActivityAt` moves forward with `$max`, **never `$set`** — a
    backdated touch must not drag it backwards and float an old lead to the top
    of the stalest-first Hot Leads panel. Best-effort and post-commit: the
    activity row is the durable artifact and is already written.
  - `source` is stamped `internal`. The schema default is `migration`, so
    omitting it would label a producer's own note as imported data.

  ## Response `201`
  ```json
  {
    "activity": {
      "id": "6a74...910",
      "type": "call",
      "summary": "Call logged",
      "occurredAt": "2026-08-06T09:56:53.463Z",
      "userName": "Pat Producer"
    },
    "leadLastActivityAt": "2026-08-06T09:56:53.463Z"
  }
  ```
  `userName` is whoever wrote the row, not the lead's producer — this endpoint
  needs only `leads:write`, which a Branch Manager and a CSR both hold. The
  field was `producerName` (over `Activity.producerId`) until PAC-65 renamed it.
  `activity` is the same `LeadDetailActivity` shape `GET /leads/:id` returns, so
  the timeline splices it in with no mapping layer.

  ## Errors
  - `400` — a system-generated `type`, a `note` with no `summary`, or a summary
    over 500 chars.
  - `403` — missing `leads:write`.
  - `404` — unknown, malformed, or another producer's `leadId`.

  > Run **List Hot Leads** first: it captures `hotLeadId`.
}
